utput as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n88,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Treatment Combinations**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nTreatment combinations refer to the various configurations of factor levels used in an experimental design to assess their effects on a response variable. They are integral to the experimental design process, particularly in factorial designs, where multiple factors are tested simultaneously to evaluate their individual and interactive effects on outcomes. Treatment combinations allow researchers to systematically explore the influence of different factors and their levels on the response variable, thereby facilitating a comprehensive understanding of the underlying processes.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a factorial design, treatment combinations can be represented mathematically as \\( Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\), where:\n- \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th level of factor A,\n- \\( \\beta_j \\) represents the effect of the \\( j \\)-th level of factor B,\n- \\( (\\alpha\\beta)_{ij} \\) represents the interaction effect between factors A and B,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nTreatment combinations are structured based on the levels of factors involved in the experiment. In a \\( 2^k \\) factorial design, for example, each factor can take on two levels (e.g., high and low), leading to \\( 2^k \\) unique treatment combinations. The layout typically includes a matrix where rows represent different treatment combinations and columns represent the factors and their levels.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of treatment combinations relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The responses for different treatment combinations should be independent.\n2. Normality: The response variable should be approximately normally distributed for each treatment combination.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance of the response variable should be constant across all treatment combinations.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nTreatment combinations are applicable in experiments where multiple factors influence a response variable, particularly when interactions between factors are of interest. They are selected based on the research objectives, the number of factors, and the levels of each factor. Factorial designs, including full and fractional designs, are commonly used to explore treatment combinations.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of treatment combinations typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of the effects of factors and their interactions on the response variable. The procedure includes:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Calculating the ANOVA table, including sums of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and F-statistics.\n3. Conducting post-hoc tests if necessary to identify specific differences between treatment combinations.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Treatment combinations allow for the efficient exploration of multiple factors and their interactions.\n- They provide a structured approach to experimental design, facilitating the identification of optimal conditions.\n- The use of factorial designs can lead to significant savings in time and resources compared to one-factor-at-a-time experiments.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The complexity of analyzing interactions can increase with the number of factors and levels, making interpretation challenging.\n- The requirement for a larger number of experimental runs can lead to increased costs and resource demands.\n- Assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance may not always hold, potentially affecting the validity of results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nTreatment combinations are closely related to factorial designs, including full factorial and fractional factorial designs. Alternatives include response surface methodology (RSM) for optimization and Taguchi methods for robust design.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing treatment combinations, researchers should consider the feasibility of conducting all combinations, the potential for confounding factors, and the need for randomization to mitigate bias. Proper planning of the experimental layout is crucial to ensure valid and reliable results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software such as R, SAS, and Minitab can be utilized to design experiments involving treatment combinations, perform ANOVA, and analyze the results. These tools often provide built-in functions for generating factorial designs and conducting statistical analyses.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nTreatment combinations are widely used in various fields, including agriculture (to assess the effects of fertilizers and irrigation methods), pharmaceuticals (to evaluate drug interactions), and manufacturing (to optimize production processes). For instance, in a chemical process experiment, different treatment combinations of temperature and pressure may be tested to determine their effects on yield and purity. In an eye focus time experiment, various configurations of factors such as lighting, distance, and lens type may be analyzed to assess their impact on focus time.\",22\r\n2055,3^2 DESIGN,\"The 3^2 design is a factorial design with two factors, each at three levels, used for modeling responses in experiments.\",2\r\n507,RESPONSE VALUES,\"**Comprehensive Summary of RESPONSE VALUES**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nResponse values are the quantitative outcomes measured in an experimental study, serving as the primary indicators of the effects of various treatments or factors being investigated. They are essential for evaluating the performance of different experimental conditions and for drawing conclusions about the relationships between factors and their impacts on the outcomes.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nIn the context of statistical modeling, response values can be represented as \\( Y \\), where \\( Y \\) is a function of the factors \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\) being studied. A general linear model can be expressed as:\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the coefficients for each factor, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nResponse values are collected through various experimental designs, such as completely randomized designs, factorial designs, or block designs. The structure of the design dictates how treatments are assigned to experimental units and how response values are measured.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of response values relies on several assumptions, including:\n1. Independence of observations: Each response value is independent of others.\n2. Normality: The distribution of response values should be approximately normal, especially for small sample sizes.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance of response values should be constant across different levels of the factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nResponse values are applicable in any experimental context where the effects of treatments or factors need to be quantified. Selection criteria for measuring response values include the nature of the research question, the type of data available, and the specific experimental design employed.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of response values typically involves statistical techniques such as Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine if there are significant differences among group means. The procedure includes:\n1. Formulating hypotheses (null and alternative).\n2. Calculating the ANOVA table, including sums of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and F-statistics.\n3. Conducting post-hoc tests if necessary to identify specific group differences.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Response values provide a clear quantitative measure of the effects of treatments.\n- They facilitate the comparison of different experimental conditions.\n- The analysis of response values can yield insights into the relationships between factors and outcomes.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Response values may be influenced by uncontrolled variables, leading to confounding.\n- The assumptions required for valid statistical analysis may not always hold, potentially invalidating results.\n- Outliers in response values can disproportionately affect the analysis.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nResponse values are integral to various experimental designs and statistical methods, including factorial designs, response surface methodology, and regression analysis. Alternatives to traditional response value analysis may include non-parametric methods when assumptions of normality or homogeneity of variance are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing response value measurements, researchers should ensure proper randomization, replication, and control of extraneous variables. The choice of response variable should align with the research objectives and be measurable with precision.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nCommon statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Minitab provide tools for analyzing response values through ANOVA and other statistical methods, facilitating the interpretation of experimental results.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nResponse values are widely used in various fields, including agriculture (e.g., crop yield), medicine (e.g., patient recovery rates), and engineering (e.g., product performance metrics). They are fundamental in quality control processes, clinical trials, and any experimental research aimed at understanding the effects of different treatments or conditions.\",1\r\n2052,FACTOR LEVELS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Factor Levels**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nFactor levels are the specific values or settings that factors can take in an experimental design. They represent the different conditions under which the experiment is conducted, allowing researchers to assess the effects of varying the independent variables on the response variable. The purpose of defining factor levels is to systematically explore the relationship between factors and outcomes, facilitating the identification of optimal conditions and interactions among factors.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn the context of a factorial design, the response variable \\( Y \\) can be modeled as a function of the factors and their levels. The general form of the model can be expressed as:\n\\[\nY = \\mu + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\beta_i X_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{j=i+1}^{k} \\beta_{ij} X_i X_j + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the main effects of the factors, \\( \\beta_{ij} \\) are the interaction effects, \\( X_i \\) and \\( X_j \\) are the factor levels, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nFactor levels are integral to the design layout of experiments, particularly in factorial designs. Each factor can have multiple levels, and the combination of these levels across factors creates a grid or matrix of experimental conditions. For example, in a 2^3 factorial design, there are three factors, each at two levels, resulting in \\( 2^3 = 8 \\) unique experimental conditions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of conclusions drawn from experiments involving factor levels relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: Each experimental unit must be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals of the model should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among groups should be approximately equal.\n4. Linearity: The relationship between factors and the response should be linear.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nFactor levels are used in various experimental designs, particularly when researchers aim to understand the effects of multiple factors simultaneously. They are applicable in situations where:\n- The researcher has control over the factors.\n- The goal is to optimize a response variable.\n- Interactions between factors are of interest.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of experiments involving factor levels typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of the effects of factors and their levels on the response variable. The procedure includes:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Conducting ANOVA to assess the significance of main effects and interactions.\n3. Performing post-hoc tests if necessary to identify specific differences between factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Factor levels allow for a systematic exploration of the effects of multiple factors.\n- They facilitate the identification of interactions between factors, which can be critical for understanding complex systems.\n- The use of factorial designs can lead to more efficient experiments, requiring fewer runs than testing factors individually.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The complexity of analysis increases with the number of factors and levels, potentially leading to difficulties in interpretation.\n- If the number of levels is too high, it may lead to an unmanageable number of experimental conditions.\n- Assumptions of ANOVA must be met for valid results; violations can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nFactor levels are closely related to factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, and response surface methodology. Alternatives include one-factor-at-a-time (OFAT) approaches, which may be simpler but do not account for interactions between factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing experiments with factor levels, researchers should carefully consider the selection of levels to ensure they are representative of the range of interest. Additionally, randomization and replication should be employed to enhance the reliability of results.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and Minitab, provide tools for designing experiments and analyzing data involving factor levels. These tools often include functions for conducting ANOVA, generating interaction plots, and performing post-hoc analyses.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nFactor levels are commonly used in industrial experiments, agricultural studies, and clinical trials, where researchers seek to optimize processes, improve product quality, or evaluate treatment effects. For instance, in a study to optimize fertilizer application, different types of fertilizers (factors) may be tested at various application rates (levels) to determine their effect on crop yield.\",1\r\n2053,FIGURE 9.1,\"Figure 9.1 illustrates the treatment combinations in a 3^2 design, showing how two factors can be combined at three levels each.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n109,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,FACTORIAL EXPERIMENT,Treatment combinations are the various setups tested in a factorial experiment,60\r\n2765,3^2 DESIGN,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,The 3^2 design involves specific treatment combinations that are used to analyze the effects of two factors,24\r\n549,RESPONSE VALUES,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,Response values are derived from the different treatment combinations in an experiment,23\r\n2769,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,FACTOR LEVELS,Factor levels are combined in treatment combinations to analyze their effects on the response,23\r\n2763,FIGURE 9.1,3^2 DESIGN,Figure 9.1 visually represents the treatment combinations used in the 3^2 design,3\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insigh